@@@ hint
Scan pattern and text with two pointers. Characters and `?` simply advance both. When you meet a `*`, remember where it is in the pattern and where the text was at that moment, then first try letting it match the **empty** sequence.
@@@ hint
On a mismatch, if a `*` was seen, backtrack: let the last `*` absorb **one more** text character (`t = ++mark; p = star + 1;`). Only the most recent `*` ever needs backtracking, which gives O(|p| * |t|) worst case. At the end of the text, skip any trailing `*`s and succeed only if the pattern is exhausted too.
@@@ solution_code
#include <stddef.h>

int wildcard_match(const char *pattern, const char *text)
{
    const char *p = pattern, *t = text;
    const char *star = NULL;    /* position of the last '*' seen in the pattern */
    const char *mark = NULL;    /* text position that '*' currently extends to */

    if (p == NULL || t == NULL)
        return 0;

    while (*t != '\0') {
        if (*p == '?' || (*p != '*' && *p == *t)) {
            p++;                    /* single-character match */
            t++;
        } else if (*p == '*') {
            star = p++;             /* try: '*' matches the empty sequence */
            mark = t;
        } else if (star != NULL) {
            p = star + 1;           /* backtrack: '*' swallows one more char */
            t = ++mark;
        } else {
            return 0;               /* mismatch and no '*' to fall back on */
        }
    }
    while (*p == '*')               /* trailing stars match the empty rest */
        p++;
    return *p == '\0';
}
@@@ explanation_md
**Approach: greedy two-pointer matching with single-star backtracking.**
- Literal characters and `?` advance both pointers.
- At a `*`, remember the pattern position (`star`) and the text position (`mark`), and first assume the star matches nothing.
- On a mismatch, go back to the **most recent** star and let it consume one more text character.
- When the text is exhausted, only `*`s may remain in the pattern.

Why only the last star needs backtracking: once the part of the pattern **between** two stars has matched, an earlier star never has to grow. Any longer match for it can be simulated by the later star. That removes the exponential branching of the recursive solution.

**Complexity.** Worst case O(|p| * |t|): each backtrack advances `mark` by one, and each restart re-scans at most |p| pattern characters. Typical filters are close to linear. Space O(1), with no recursion.

A DP alternative: `dp[i][j]` = pattern prefix `i` matches text prefix `j`, with `dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j] || dp[i][j-1]` for `*`. That is also O(|p| * |t|), and with a rolling row it needs O(|t|) memory, which is fine on a PC but heavy on an MCU.

**Common mistakes**
- Plain recursion (`match(p+1, t) || match(p, t+1)` for `*`). It is exponential on `*a*a*a...b`, and for a 20000-character text it also risks a stack overflow from deep recursion.
- A full `(|p|+1) x (|t|+1)` DP table as a local array: 4 MB, which overflows a 1 MB (or 8 KB MCU) stack.
- Matching a substring instead of the whole text (forgetting to require `*p == '\0'` at the end).
- Treating `?` as "zero or one" character, or letting it match the end of the text (`"?"` vs `""` must fail).
- Not collapsing or skipping trailing `*`s, so `"abc*"` fails on `"abc"`.

**Interview follow-ups**
- Add character classes like `[0-9]` and an escape `\*` for a literal star. Which parts of the algorithm change?
- How do production shells avoid quadratic behavior? (Matching the literal segments between stars with `strstr`, and the "last star only" property.)
- Compare this with a regular-expression engine. Why can backtracking regex engines be exponential (ReDoS), while this glob matcher cannot?
- The text arrives as a stream of CAN signal names from a DBC file in flash. Can you match without random access to the text?
